================================================================================
python-modbus-poll -- Changelog 
================================================================================

Python-modbus-poll-v13 / 28.05.2026

BUGFIX: FC01 / FC02 coil and discrete input parsing was broken.
  v12 treated all response data as 16-bit register pairs. FC01/FC02
  responses are bit-packed (8 coils per byte, LSB first) so the v12
  parser would misinterpret or error on these. v13 detects FC01/FC02,
  unpacks bits correctly, trims padding bits to the requested count,
  and routes the result to a dedicated decode_coils() display function.

IMPROVEMENT: Instead of s.recv(260) that assumes the full response
  arrives in one TCP read and can silently truncate on slow or
  fragmented links, v13 reads the 7-byte MBAP header first, extracts
  the Length field, then loops until exactly pdu_length - 1 additional
  bytes are received. The -1 subtracts the Unit ID byte already
  consumed by the header read.

NEW: --retries N (default 3). Retry attempts on any socket/IO failure.
  Each failed attempt waits 1 second before retrying.

NEW: --timeout N (default 5). Per-attempt socket timeout in seconds.
  Useful for slow serial gateways which may need --timeout 10 or higher.

NEW: --interval N. Poll continuously every N seconds. TCP connection is
  kept alive between polls and reconnected automatically on failure.
  Ctrl-C stops cleanly with total poll count reported.

NEW: --count is now validated against Modbus specification limits.
  FC01/FC02 max 2000 coils, FC03/FC04 max 125 registers. If exceeded,
  the value is clamped with a printed warning before sending.

NEW: BCD decode shown per register in --verbose mode. Each 16-bit
  register is decoded as packed BCD (4 nibbles = 4 decimal digits).
  If any nibble is > 9 the register is flagged as invalid BCD.
  Common in older PLCs: Siemens S5, Mitsubishi, Omron.
  Example: 0x1234 -> 1234

NEW: IPv4 decode shown per 32-bit pair in --verbose mode. Two
  consecutive registers decoded as packed IPv4 (hi/lo byte per
  register, ABCD order).
  Example: r0=0xC0A8, r1=0x0101 -> 192.168.1.1

NEW: Unix epoch -> datetime shown in --verbose mode. All 32-bit and
  64-bit integer interpretations are checked against a plausible
  year 2000-2100 window and displayed as UTC datetime if plausible.
  64-bit values are also checked as millisecond epochs, common in
  Java-originated industrial systems.

NEW: 64-bit types FLOAT64, INT64, UINT64 decoded automatically in
  --verbose mode whenever 4 or more registers are polled, in four
  word-order formats: ABCDEFGH, GHEFCDAB, BADCFEHG, HGFEDCBA.

NEW: UTF-16-LE added alongside the existing UTF-16-BE in the
  multi-register summary. Byte order genuinely changes decoded text
  for UTF-16, unlike ASCII/UTF-8 where endian swapping is meaningless.

RENAME: --analyze renamed to --verbose. Behaviour unchanged.

